> SP walks the span table $76A0 (POP BC: the length, POP HL: the buffer, POP DE: the screen). For each span: patches the LD BC at CopyLineEnd with 32 - the length, writes JP CopyLineEnd ($C3 $14 $95 at $94C8-$94CE) into the LDI chain at CopyChain + 2 x the length (the place saved at CopySpanRestore), the LD E at CopyLineE with the screen address's low byte; copies 8 lines (LDIs, then HL + 32 - length, E back, D + 1); puts ED A0 ED back. SpanCount spans; interrupts off meanwhile. Called by UpdateAttributes every pass and by StageEntry.
> As in stage 1 at $9ADA, the same code; the operands at $94BC-$94D3 are its own labels and the bytes $14 $95 the low and high bytes of CopyLineEnd.
> Witness: L1 8033 (rzx-r2, s2-right, stageload).
$94B5 CopySpan
$94D8 CopyChain ; 30 LDIs, cut by the JP patched over the one after the span's length
$9514 CopyLineEnd ; LD BC,n: n = 32 - the length
$9518 CopyLineE ; LD E,n: n = the span's screen address's low byte
$951E CopySpanRestore ; LD HL,n: n = where the JP was put

@module rooms
$953E b Grid0 9 ; Grid 0's record (the temple): 69 x 9 blocks, the start window at 0, 0, the room table Grid0Rooms, map offset 0, 6 rooms a grid row
> A grid record, 9 bytes, as stage 1's: +0 the width and +1 the height in blocks, +2/+3 the start window's block column and row (StartGrid; StageEntry sets +2 = $37 for the first build and puts 0 back), +4/+5 the room table, +6/+7 the grid's offset in the map section of StageGraphics, +8 the rooms a grid row. Unlike stage 1's block the three records come first, then the three room tables, then the placement lists.
> The room table, one byte a room: the exits, bit 0 right, bit 1 left, bit 2 up, bit 3 down (the edge checks).
> Read and partly written (the start window) in s2-right and rzx-r2: 146 and 189 of the map's 201 bytes read. The file map.txt holds the records, their room tables and the placement lists (tools/res_stage2.py); tools/fig_rooms.py draws the map and every room from it (docs/img/maps/stage2.png, stage2-rooms.png).
$9547 Grid1 ; Grid 1 (the hydra's caves): 47 x 15 blocks, start 0, 0, map offset $026D, 4 rooms a grid row (8 rooms; EnterGrid1, LeaveGrid2)
$9550 Grid2 ; Grid 2 (Medusa's room): 14 x 9 blocks, one room, map offset $052E (EnterGrid2)
$9559 Grid0Rooms ; 6 rooms in a row: the exits of each
$955F Grid1Rooms ; 8 rooms, 4 a row
$9567 Grid2Rooms ; 1 room, no exits
$9568 Grid0Placements ; grid 0's 18 placements: the kind (bit 7: done), X and Y in cells over the whole grid; $FF $FF $FF ends
> LoadRoomActors puts each placement not done that lies in the room's window into a free actor record with its kind's row of ActorKinds. Grid 0: the urns (kinds 16-19), the pools' ripples (20), the statues (0, 1, 14, 15), the harpy's lamp, flame and rise (2, 13, 3).
$95A1 Grid1Placements ; grid 1's 30: urns, the pool (kinds 4, 5), the hydra's heads (7-10), the drops (6)
$95FE Grid2Placements ; grid 2's 2: Medusa and her head (kinds 11, 12)
@res stage2/map.bin

@module actors
$9607 b ActorKinds 9 ; The 21 placement kinds, 9 bytes each: the Y offset, the handler, 5 bytes for the record's +8 to +$0C, the record's +$0F
> As stage 1's: LoadRoomActors ($A499) builds an actor from a placement with its kind's row: +1 Y = the placement's Y x 8 + the offset, +6/+7 the handler, +8 to +$0C (the counter, the flags, the state, the item, the attribute), +$0F (bit 0: kept over a room change). The handlers: 0 KneelingStatueActor, 1, 14, 15 WarriorStatueActor, 2 HarpyLampActor, 3 HarpyRiseActor, 4 and 5 PoolActor, 6 DropActor, 7-10 HydraHead1Actor, HydraHead2Actor, HydraHead3Actor, HydraHead1LowActor, 11 MedusaActor, 12 MedusaHeadActor, 13 HarpyFlameActor, 16-19 StrikeableActor, 20 WaterActor.
